  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef defines a pair of positive integers (a,b)(a, b)(a,b) to be a Oneful&nbsp;Pair\\text{Oneful Pair}Oneful&nbsp;Pair, if\n\na+b+(a⋅b)=111a + b + (a \\cdot b) = 111a+b+(a⋅b)=111\n\nFor example, (1,55)(1, 55)(1,55) is a Oneful&nbsp;Pair\\text{Oneful Pair}Oneful&nbsp;Pair, since 1+55+(1⋅55)=56+55=1111 + 55 + (1 \\cdot 55) = 56 + 55 = 1111+55+(1⋅55)=56+55=111.\n\nBut (1,56)(1, 56)(1,56) is not a Oneful&nbsp;Pair\\text{Oneful Pair}Oneful&nbsp;Pair, since 1+56+(1⋅56)=57+56=113≠1111 + 56 + (1 \\cdot 56) = 57 + 56 = 113 \\neq 1111+56+(1⋅56)=57+56=113=111.\n\nGiven two positive integers aaa and bbb, output `Yes` if they are a Oneful&nbsp;Pair\\text{Oneful Pair}Oneful&nbsp;Pair. And `No` otherwise.\n\n\nInput Format\nThe only line of input contains two space-separated integers aaa and bbb.\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nOutput `Yes`, if (a,b)(a, b)(a,b) form a Oneful&nbsp;Pair\\text{Oneful Pair}Oneful&nbsp;Pair. Output `No` if they do not.\n\n\nYou may print each character of `Yes` and `No` in uppercase or lowercase (for example, `yes`, `yEs`, `Yes` will be considered identical).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤a,b≤10001 \\leq a, b \\leq 10001≤a,b≤1000"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
